// A borrow chain longer than the marking fixpoint will follow (cycle 0.5.7).
//
// WHICH BINDINGS HOLD A BORROW IS DATAFLOW, and D-116 settled that it is computed
// by marking to a fixpoint rather than in one pass. The rounds are bounded, because
// an unbounded loop in a checker is not a checker — and what happens at the bound
// is the part that matters.
//
// IT REFUSES RATHER THAN REPORTING WHAT IT HAD. An analysis that quietly hands back
// partial results is the under-reporting the fixpoint exists to prevent, arrived at
// by a different road: the answer would look like "no escapes found" and mean "we
// stopped counting".
//
// The chain below is written in REVERSE — each link is assigned from the one after
// it, and only the last takes an actual borrow — so a round propagates exactly one
// link and settling needs as many rounds as there are links.

// expect-error: NITPICK-BORROW-010
